{
  "title": "CoreBMC Power Fault Error Map (SC platform family)",
  "scope": "SC-2200R, SC-2400H, SC-4800G, SC-1150S, SC-3300M running CoreBMC 3.x",
  "problem": "A node fails to power on, drops unexpectedly, or logs a rail fault. The BMC records a rail-specific error pattern in the system event log and in the autolog bundle. The pattern names the rail that failed, and the rail determines which components are in scope — which is the difference between a targeted repair and swapping parts until it stops.",
  "root_cause": "A power rail failing to come up in sequence, or collapsing at runtime. The failure originates in one of three places: the supply feeding the rail, the board-mounted regulator producing it, or the component drawing from it. The rail name narrows this to a handful of candidates. Memory rails narrow it further, to specific DIMM slots, because each PVDDQ rail feeds a known set of channels.",
  "solution": "1. Capture the full SEL and the autolog bundle BEFORE removing AC. Rail events are held in volatile state on some firmware levels and are lost on power removal.\n2. Match the logged error pattern against this map to identify the rail and its suspect components.\n3. For a memory rail, the map gives the DIMM slots that rail feeds — reseat or replace within those slots only.\n4. Remove AC for 60 seconds, then retry power-on at minimum configuration.\n5. Add components back in groups, retesting after each, until the fault reproduces.\n6. A rail fault that reproduces at minimum configuration is a board or supply fault, not a component fault.",
  "suggestions": "Do not start by replacing the system board. On this platform family the majority of rail faults resolve to a single component drawing out of spec, a partially seated module, or a supply that is not sharing load. The board is the last candidate, not the first.\nCheck whether both supplies are present, matched and sharing before investigating any rail — an unbalanced pair produces rail events that look like component faults.\nIf two or more unrelated rails assert in the same second, suspect the supply or the sequencer, not the components.",
  "source_url": "",
